I was referring to spammers who promote adult websites, and newsgroups
where the denizens exchange adult material.  Although, as you said, a
feed's full list of newsgroup names are equally available to the spammers
as to those wishing to read news, so a spammer theoretically could spam
everywhere, it is evident by inspecting any newsgroup's postings that they
do not. There is almost no spam in rec.games.roguelike.nethack, for
instance, and what little there is occasionally is nothing compared to the
vast wasteland to be found in any alt.binaries group with even a slightly
risque name.

Therefore the abovementioned newsgroup denizens have developed the habit of
using less obvious groups for their exchanges of questionable material.
alt.oyp.sworp, for example, is a pretty impenetrable newsgroup name -- just
from the name, it is very hard to imagine what postings there might be
about. (I don't recommend going there, by the way. It is not pleasant.)

Perhaps now the meaning of my remarks becomes clear. Consider a spammer who
is currently flooding the lamented alt.binaries.pictures.erotica.teens. His
website, presumably, contains material that is of interest to those who are
partial to underage (or barely-of-age, as the spammer may put it) persons
engaged in reproductive activity (activity that is, however, unlikely to
result in progeny. At least one hopes so). But the competition in abpet is
fierce, with hundreds of spammers all crowing loudly about their
disreputable yet lucrative websites. And non-spammer postings have become
scarce; the spammer has begun to doubt (quite reasonably) that anyone is
actually reading the group. So his advertising is for naught.

But what's this? A message is crossposted to abpet AND alt.oyp.sworp! The
spammer concludes that there must be readers of alt.oyp.sworp who would
also be interested in his material!  He peruses the filth already being
posted there and confirms his suspicion.  Hooray, new readers! Less
competition! Spamming operations commence immediately.

The advanced spammer has robots that scan the Newsgroups: header of all
postings in his current target groups automatically, looking for new
newsgroups. And he probably won't bother to confirm that the new potential
target is actually a good target.

So. I hope I have made my meaning more clear than before. Obviously this
situation is not a risk when crossposting between rgrn and misc.misc, since
neither is a group with adult subject matter. I reiterate that I personally
have no problem with your crossposting in this situation.

Now that I have shined a flashlight at the ugly underbelly of Usenet,
perhaps your opinion of me has dropped a few million points. I would not be
surprised; I am not proud that I know about such things.  Nevertheless, I
am not a liar.  (Though my honesty here has probably damaged my reputation
even more than lying would.)

